BUG TO FIX: adding days across the year-100 boundary gives a result off by one day when the addition passes through February.
Repro:
  const d = DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 61 });
  d.toISODate(); // currently "0100-03-01", expected "0100-03-02"
(Reason: 0099-12-31 + 61 days = 0100-03-02, because year 100 is not a leap year while year 2000 is.)

ROOT CAUSE: in /luxon/src/impl/util.js, `objToLocalTS(obj)` calls `Date.UTC(obj.year, ...)`; JS maps years 0..99 to 19xx. The current workaround is:

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
  }

This only fixes the year, not the month/day overflow: `DateTime#plus` passes an un-normalized day (e.g. day 92 for 0099-12-31 + 61 days), so Date.UTC normalizes it using year 1999/2000 (2000 is a leap year) before the year is corrected, producing the wrong day.

APPLY THIS EXACT CHANGE (keep surrounding code and prettier style; only replace that if-block):

  // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
  if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
    d = new Date(d);
    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
    // so if obj.year is in 99, but obj.day makes it roll over into year 100,
    // the calculations done by Date.UTC are using year 2000 - which is a leap year - and not year 100
    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
  }

VERIFY (all must pass):
1. `cd /luxon && npx jest test/datetime test/impl test/zones 2>&1 | tail -30` - all must pass. Pay special attention to test/datetime/math.test.js ("DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier", "DateTime#minus works across the 100 barrier"), test/datetime/create.test.js (low-year cases) and test/datetime/zone.test.js ("0001-01-01").
2. Write a scratch script at /tmp/check2.js (OUTSIDE the repo) and run it with `cd /luxon && npx babel-node /tmp/check2.js` (if babel-node is missing, use another way to import /luxon/src/luxon.js, e.g. `npx jest --rootDir /tmp` with a scratch test file, or node with @babel/register). It must assert:
   - DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 61 }).toISODate() === "0100-03-02"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 2 }).toISODate() === "0100-01-02"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0100-01-02").minus({ day: 2 }).toISODate() === "0099-12-31"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0001-01-01", { zone: "UTC" }).toISODate() === "0001-01-01"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 60 }).toISODate() === "0100-03-01"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0096-12-31").plus({ day: 61 }).toISODate() === "0097-03-02" (year 96 is a leap year: Jan 31 + Feb 29 = 60 days, so +61 = Mar 2)
   - DateTime.fromISO("0100-03-01").minus({ day: 61 }).toISODate() === "0099-12-31"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 61 }).toJSDate().getTime() equals the value computed independently via `Date.UTC(100, 2, 2)` minus `Date.UTC(99, 11, 31)` days check (i.e. just confirm the resulting ISO date is 0100-03-02 and the DateTime is valid).
   - DateTime.fromObject({ year: 99, month: 12, day: 31 }).plus({ day: 61 }).toISODate() === "0100-03-02"
   - DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ day: 61 }).toISODate() must equal DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ days: 61 }).toISODate()
3. Then run the FULL suite: `cd /luxon && npx jest 2>&1 | tail -30`. Note: another agent may be mid-edit on src/interval.js; if you see failures only in test/interval/*.tes

